Preparation of HKUST-1 film with 3D-ordered macroporous structure: Clear HKUST-1 precursor solutions were prepared according to the literature.[9] In a typical preparation, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(1.22{\mathrm{g}})$ and 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{3}\mathrm{\bf{BTC}}$ ; $\mathbf{0.58\:g})$ were dissolved in DMSO $(5.0\mathrm{mL})$ ). The clear precursor solution was heated to $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then was infiltrated into the COOH-terminated polystyrene colloidal crystals templates under vacuum. After evaporation of the solvent at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ , the templates were removed by immersion in THF solution. This treatment was repeated several times, and the resulting samples were dried at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight.
Synthesis of polystyrene colloidal crystal templates: Non-crosslinked, monodisperse polystyrene spheres with carboxylic acid terminated surfaces were synthesized using an emulsifier-free emulsion polymerization technique. In a typical synthesis, a three-necked, $200\mathrm{mL}$ round-bottomed flask was filled with water $\mathrm{(80~mL)}$ ) and heated to $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before styrene $(7.0\mathrm{g})$ and methacrylic acid $(0.35{\mathrm{g}})$ were added under intensive stirring. Pure nitrogen was bubbled to deaerate the mixture for $30\mathrm{min}$ . In a separate $25\mathrm{mL}$ polyethylene bottle, sodium hydroxide $(0.024\ \mathrm{g)}$ and sodium carbonate $(0.024\:\mathrm{g})$ was dissolved in water $(5\mathrm{mL})$ , and the solution was added to the former solution which was reheated to $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Potassium persulfate initiator $(0.03\:\mathrm{g})$ was added to water $(5\mathrm{mL})$ and the solution was deaerated for $10\mathrm{min}$ . After the initiator was added to the total solution, nitrogen was passed through the flask for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The temperature was kept at $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ . After alternating centrifugation and dispersion using water several times to expunge residues, monodisperse COOH-terminated polystyrene particles $\left(330\mathrm{nm}\right)$ were obtained and fully dispersed in water with a weight concentration of about $0.1\%$ ; these were transferred into clean $7\mathrm{mL}$ vials for the formation of colloidal crystal templates. A clean glass slide was put into each vial vertically for colloidal crystal growth.[15] After complete volatilization of water, COOH-terminated polystyrene
## Experimental Section
Characterization: XRD measurements were performed on a Bruker D8 Advance X-Ray powder diffractometer. TEM images were obtained using a JEM 2010 high-resolution transmission electronic microscope at an acceleration voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}.$ SEM images were obtained using a field emission scanning electron microscopy (ESSEM) on a JEOL JSM-5400 system at an accelerating voltage of $8\mathrm{kV.}$ Optical spectra and photos of the HKUST-1 films were acquired with an Ocean Optics USB2000 fiber optic spectrophotometer coupled to an optical microscope. ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption– desorption isotherm measurements were carried out on a QuadraSorb SI instrument at $77\mathrm{K}$ . Prior to the measurement, the sample was degassed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{n}$ in the vacuum line. The FTIR spectra were measured on a Spectrum One FTIR spectrometer (Perkin– Elmer) by the KBr pellet method.
